WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.640
Let’s say you really need
to find reliable information

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.840
about the best diet for
high blood pressure,

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:10.000
or heart disease,
or diabetes.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:11.360
Where do you go?

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.920
Do you go to a website
sponsored by Big Pharma

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.720
that wants to sell you
pills to fix your problem?

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.760
Or, do you want
to treat the cause?

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.280
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.800
with the latest peer-
reviewed research

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.840
on the best ways to eat
healthy and live longer.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.280
Today we take a close
look at the mighty power

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:34.800
of vitamin C.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.720
And we start with
the risks and benefits

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.186
of using vitamin C for
depression and anxiety.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.160
“Emerging evidence suggests that
higher daily intake of ﬁber-rich

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.080
fruits and vegetables is associated
with lower incidences of anxiety

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:52.000
in adults and at the same time,
greater happiness, higher life

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.040
satisfaction, and greater
emotional well-being.”

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.760
So, “persuading people to
consume more fruits and vegetables

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.040
may not only benefit their
physical health in the long-run,

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.320
but also their mental
well-being in the short-run.”

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.320
Fruit consumption, for example,
has protective association

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.200
with leading killers like
heart disease, stroke, diabetes,

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.040
and cancer, as well as
depression. The question is why?

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.080
Several different mechanisms
have been proposed.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:21.440
For example, it may be the
antioxidants and anti-inflammatory

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:24.400
properties of produce, scavenging
the free radicals that are

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.240
involved in some of the
inflammation associated with depression.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.520
If that’s the case, what about
just taking vitamin C supplements?

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.280
The brain has some of the highest
levels of vitamin C in the body;

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:38.000
so, the thought is if
we take extra vitamin C,

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.440
it may have some sort of
therapeutic role in brain diseases,

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.320
especially given that
it’s not just an antioxidant,

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:47.120
but also has other critical
functions in the brain such as

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.160
helping to build
neurotransmitters like dopamine.

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.800
But you don’t know
whether it actually helps,

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.520
until you... put it to the test.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:00.000
One study found a beneficial
effect of adding vitamin C as an

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.040
adjunct treatment to an
antidepressant, while another study

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.840
found no benefit from
vitamin C supplementation.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.360
So, there are mixed results
for vitamin C and depression.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.000
Here’s another study that
found no benefit when it came to

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.400
depression, but those
randomized to vitamin C instead of

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:19.600
placebo pills did show a
significant decrease in anxiety level.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:22.640
And this wasn’t only
seen in this study.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:25.200
The effects of oral
vitamin C supplementation

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.360
on anxiety in students:

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.000
a double-blind, randomized,
placebo-controlled trial.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.160
High school students were
given 500mg a day or placebo.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:36.720
500mg of vitamin C is
about what you’d find

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.640
in five oranges, four
yellow kiwifruit or guavas,

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:42.560
or one and a half
yellow bell peppers.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.600
Within just two weeks, the vitamin C
reduced anxiety compared to

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.800
placebo, along with providing
a significant drop in heart rate.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.920
Given these data showing
vitamin C may have

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.440
anxiety-reducing effect,
researchers sought to find out

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.280
if only a single dose could
acutely affect emotional states.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.800
And indeed, within only two
hours of taking some C,

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:08.320
subjects experienced a
significant drop in anxiety

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:10.880
compared to placebo,
though only among those

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:14.400
who started out the most
anxious in the first place.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.280
As a bonus, vitamin C
supplementation may lower

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.760
your blood pressure a
few points, but whole fruits

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.480
and vegetables can do
the same thing and may even

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:25.440
do it better for those
who need it the most.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.800
As I’ve shown previously,
even just adding two servings

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:30.960
of fruits and vegetables a
day can have psychological

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.280
well-being benefits
within only two weeks.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.920
And you won’t be put at risk
of kidney stones like you would

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.560
with vitamin C supplements,
though that appears to be

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:41.920
only a problem in men.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:44.720
Here’s a fun fact.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:46.480
There are now experiments
that can show

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.534
how much vitamin C our
body absorbs and excretes.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.087
Here’s the story.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:55.120
For many years, the RDA, the
recommended daily allowance

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.320
for vitamins, was just based
on preventing deficiency,

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:00.160
with a margin of safety,

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:04.240
but the miniscule amount of vitamin C,
for example, needed to avoid scurvy

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:07.520
is not necessarily the ideal
intake for optimal health.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:11.120
So what might be the
optimal intake of vitamin C?

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:14.640
Let's ask the body.
How might we do that?

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:18.640
By seeing how much the
body absorbs and excretes.

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:21.600
If you swallow 15 mg of vitamin C,
which is what you would get

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:25.920
eating about a quarter of an orange,
your body sucks up nearly 90% of it.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:29.120
But if you take a supplement
containing 1,250 mg,

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:34.000
your body seems to realize that's too much,
and so clamps down on absorption

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:38.000
at the intestinal lining level, and you
end up absorbing less than half.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:42.160
So by doing experiments where
you slowly ratchet up the intake

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.480
you can see when the body
starts to say, OK, that's enough,

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:52.160
and that magic level of intake
appears to be about 200 mg a day.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:55.040
When you take up to 200 a day
your body sucks it all up,

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:58.320
but above that your body tries
to block further absorption

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:01.920
suggesting our intestinal vitamin C
transport mechanisms have evolved

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:05.680
to fully absorb up to about
200 mg of vitamin C a day.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:11.440
In addition, vitamin C is reabsorbed in
our kidneys back into our bloodstream

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:17.280
to maintain our vitamin C blood levels up
at around 70 or 80 micromoles per liter.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:23.200
Which is what you reach at a vitamin C
intake of about 200 mg a day.

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:26.880
So even if you take 10 times as
much vitamin C supplements,

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:31.920
you know, 2000 mg a day, your
body will just pee and poop it out

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.320
to keep your blood levels
in that narrow range.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:37.040
So based on these kind of data,

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:41.840
one might propose that 200 mg is
the optimal daily intake of vitamin C.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.040
You can confirm using disease data.

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:49.920
At what daily intake of vitamin C is
there the lowest stroke risk, for example?

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:52.480
Apparently at about 200 mg a day.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:57.600
While dietary vitamin C intake was
associated with lower stroke risk,

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.480
vitamin C supplements
were not,

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:02.880
which is consistent with
the overall body of evidence

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:05.920
showing that antioxidant
supplements in general

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:08.160
don't seem to protect against
heart attacks or strokes.

00:06:08.568 --> 00:06:09.280
But wait...

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:14.560
Can you get all up to an intake of 200 mg
a day without taking supplements?

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:17.120
No problem.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:20.880
Single servings of fruits and
vegetables may have about 50 mg each,

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:24.240
so a measly 5 servings
of fruits and veggies a day

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:27.120
could get you to
ideal blood levels.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:31.040
Finally today, what
can we conclude

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:35.760
about the role of IV vitamin C
after 33 years of trials

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:38.405
involving more than
1,500 patients?

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:42.400
Studies in the 70s showed an
extraordinary survival gain

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:44.640
in terminal cancer patients
with vitamin C,

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:47.280
a simple, relatively
nontoxic therapy,

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:49.360
and so no wonder it got
a lot of attention

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:52.560
especially when reported by
a world-renowned scientist,

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:53.360
Linus Pauling,

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:58.240
but studies like this in the
1980s found no such benefit

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:00.880
and so alas they were left
with the inevitable conclusion

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:02.480
that the apparent
positive results

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:05.200
in the original study were
the product of bias

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:06.880
rather than treatment
effectiveness.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:11.760
In the 90s though,
an alternative explanation arose.

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:14.880
The disappointing 80s research
only used oral vitamin C,

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:17.760
whereas the apparently
successful 70s experiments

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:20.160
also gave vitamin C
intravenously

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:23.600
and we didn’t realize
until the 90s

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:27.600
that the same dose given IV
can lead to dramatically

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:28.880
higher levels in
the blood stream

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:29.840
than if taken
orally,

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.080
So maybe high dose vitamin C
does help in terminal cancer,

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:36.080
but maybe only when
given intravenously.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:39.840
Encouraging case reports
continue to be published.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:44.000
Here there was a regression,
remission and cure

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:46.800
documented in individual cases
of advanced kidney cancer,

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:48.160
bladder cancer,
and lymphoma.

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:52.240
But that’s three success
stories out of how many?

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:53.280
If it was three
out of a hundred,

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:55.200
or even three out
of a thousand...well okay,

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:57.840
if the treatment is
sufficiently nontoxic,

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:01.360
but there’s evidence
that IV vitamin C

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:04.720
is widely used in the
alternative medicine world,

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:08.720
as in 86% of
practitioners surveyed.

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:12.800
Just those 172
practitioners alone

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:16.640
treated about 10,000
patients a year

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:18.080
And you ask the
manufacturers,

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:20.960
and they’re selling hundreds
of thousands of vials

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:22.240
of this stuff
in the U.S.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.040
Now it’s not all being
used for cancer,

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:27.840
but presumably at least thousands
of cancer patients are being treated

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.960
every year with IV vitamin C,
making the publication

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:34.160
of three remarkable case
reports seem less impressive.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:38.480
So no matter how amazing
these cases seemed,

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:41.520
it’s possible the cancers
just spontaneously regressed

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:43.760
all on their own and it
was just a coincidence

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:46.240
that it happened after they
were given vitamin C.

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:49.760
To know for sure you have
to put it to the test.

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:53.200
To date there have been
some small pilot studies,

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:55.440
and the results so far
have been disappointing.

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:00.000
The good news is that even
insane doses of IV vitamin C

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.280
seem remarkably safe,

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:04.400
but failed in this study
of two dozen patients

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:06.560
to demonstrate
anticancer activity.

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:09.360
Similar small studies
have been published

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:10.800
all the way through
to the present

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:13.600
with tantalizing but
inconclusive results.

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:20.960
What we do know is that the present
state of cancer treatment

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:22.800
is unsatisfactory.

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:26.080
People have this perception
that chemotherapy

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:28.320
will significantly enhance
their chances for a cure,

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:31.520
but put all our cancer-killing
chemo together

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:34.400
and the overall contribution
to 5-year survival

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:36.400
is on the
order of 2%.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:38.960
All those side-effects
for 2.1%,

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:45.760
at a cost of maybe $100,000
per patient per year.

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:49.600
So it may be worth looking
deeper into therapies

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:51.120
like IV vitamin C.

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:54.080
However, the lack of
financial reward,

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:56.080
since vitamin C can’t be patented
and sold for $100,000,

00:09:57.440 --> 00:09:59.520
and bias against
alternative medicine

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:02.480
could dissuade conventional
investigators and funding agencies

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:04.720
from seriously considering
this approach.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:07.920
So decades later what
can we conclude?

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:11.520
After trials which have included
at least 1,600 patients

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:13.360
over 33 years, we
have to conclude...

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:15.840
that we still don't know

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:19.280
whether Vitamin C has any clinically
significant antitumor activity.

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:23.760
Although there is currently no
definitive evidence of benefit,

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:25.760
the Mayo Clinic randomized
controlled trials

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:29.600
did not negate the potential
benefit based on what we now know

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:32.880
about oral versus IV
routes of administration.

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:35.760
So we’re kind of
back to square one,

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:37.200
does it work
or not?

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:41.680
There are highly polarized
views on both sides,

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:44.560
but everyone’s working off
the same incomplete data.

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:47.360
What we need are carefully
controlled clinical trials.

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:49.920
The question is, what
do we do until then?

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:53.200
If it was completely nontoxic,
then one could argue-

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:54.160
what have you
got to lose?

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:58.560
But it’s not; it’s only
relatively nontoxic.

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:01.680
For example, there have
been rare but serious cases

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:03.440
of kidney injury
reported.

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:06.080
After all, if it’s
so safe,

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:07.600
why did our
bodies evolve

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:10.560
to so tightly control
against excess absorption?

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:14.880
It can also be expensive
and time-consuming.

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:18.640
Each infusion can cost 1 to 2 hundred
dollars out of pocket,

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:20.480
since insurance
doesn't pay for it.

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:25.200
which can be quite a boon for
alternative medicine practitioners.

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:28.800
About 90% of the millions
of doses of vitamin C

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:32.080
being dispensed are in
for-profit arrangements,

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:35.920
so there’s financial pressures
pushing in both directions,

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:39.200
for and against
this treatment.

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:43.280
Given the relative safety
and expense, though,

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:45.920
if controlled studies even
find a small benefit,

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:47.200
it would be
worthwhile.

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:50.240
And if they don’t, okay, fine,
the vitamin C question

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:52.320
can be put to rest
once and for all.

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:55.280
But in cancer treatment
we don’t have the luxury

00:11:55.280 --> 00:11:59.840
of jettisoning possibly effective
relatively nontoxic treatments.

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:02.080
We should revisit
promising avenues,

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:04.720
without prejudice,
and with open minds.

